there are two important data points that  we can consider to help us think about  how influencers can affect voter turnout  the first is that we know that age  makeup of the electorate is changing so  by the next presidential cycle gen Z and  Millennials are expected to make up  nearly half of the U.S voting population  the other piece that we need to consider  is just how many gen Z and Millennials  are using Tick Tock and what they're  using Tick Tock for tick tock has become  a top destination for news Pew recently  found that more than a quarter of Young  Americans under the age of 30 regularly  get their news there and so between that  and the fact that creators have in you  know there's some creators that have  enormous followings on this platform  many of them can actually draw more  eyeballs and engagement than new set  lists across the board any organization  would tell you that if you have a global  Mega star like somebody like a Taylor  Swift telling everybody go out and get  registered and make sure you have a plan  to go vote next week many people are  going to register register and micro  influencers who have very small but very  loyal very attached very dedicated  followings they can be just as powerful  at moving the needle especially in  places that are Battleground States and  we even know just in speaking to some of  the non-profits that are working on get  out the vote around the midterms that  they have seen in recent election cycles  that having influencers get involved it  just dramatically increases the number  of voter registrations that they have so  Rock the vote which is one of the groups  that we spoke to they have teamed up  with in influencer marketing firm that  they worked with ahead of the 2020  presidential election and they said that  that past collaboration helped Drive  roughly 200 000 new voter registrations  during that cycle they really build  themselves as nonpartisan and they  really focus on driving the point that  they are not out there trying to tell  you who to vote for they're just out  there trying to get you to vote in the  first place it's really difficult for  them even though they build themselves  as non partisan to disentangle  themselves from leaning one way or the  other so for example you know you look  at the influencer guidelines that they  sent to all their creators ahead of the  election they have language and they're  saying you have the opportunity this  election cycle to elect leaders who can  tackle climate change and who can  protect your access to abortion and who  can you know make sure that voting is  protected and things like that and I  think that you know anybody reading  between the lines who understands kind  of which way these kinds of issues tilt  would think those are more Progressive  causes it's a really really tricky line  for a lot of these creators and  influencers to tow because a lot of them  have uh audiences that fall across the  political Spectrum there's one  influencer for example who I was going  to speak with for the story that we put  out who has six million followers in the  State of Florida he only would speak to  Forbes on the condition that there were  no political questions asked another  creator that we spoke to for the story  who's also working with rock the a vote  he explained that he has put too much  time and too much effort into building  trust with his audience and building his  brand that it's not worth it to him to  tell people this is who you have to vote  for he's just more interested in saying  Hey I want you to get out and vote and I  want you to go do whatever whatever it  is that you're going to do but he said  that many influencers sort of know  better than to go any further than that  I spoke to somebody who used to serve in  the Obama White House she was direct  former director of digital rapid  response and now she is helping crooked  media run their vote save America  campaign ahead of the midterms and her  view was that this is telling people to  to go vote is becoming something of a  check the box that I think we're  influencers it's becoming kind of  performative we see that kind of  behavior around many other big social  and political causes that said there's  also organizations that see that there  should be some sort of a middle ground  between putting out partisan messaging  versus putting out non-partisan  messaging and gen Z for change which is  one of the groups you spoke to for the  story they're one of the groups doing  that so they have sort of in a 60 40  split they were explaining of putting  out nonpartisan messaging the kinds of  stuff you'd see from rockthevote and  vote.org encouraging people out to the  polls and the other 40 they're very  explicitly throwing their weight behind  or individual members of the leadership  team at least are very explicitly  throwing their way behind specific  candidates saying this is an issue this  is where this person stands on it this  is where that person stands on it which  direction do you want to go in they're  you know being a lot more overt about  the types of people that they're  supporting
 

